From e88909d79ac3b7c55bae45064de456cc704494b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 16:46:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Fix cross-platform sync and make the directorate
 dashboard work
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Sync was failing differently on each platform, and the directorate dashboard
had no data source that any client actually wrote. Findings below are verified
by tests/firestore/rules.test.mjs, which runs against the Firestore emulator;
running it against the previous rules gives 20 passed, 10 failed.

SECURITY RULES

The rules are enforced for Android and Web only — the desktop app uses
firebase-admin, which bypasses them entirely. That asymmetry is why the
breakage presented as "desktop works, the others don't", and why it went
unnoticed: the desktop app cannot observe it.

Denied under the old rules:
  - audit_log writes, so Android and Web produced no audit trail
  - visitor_log, purchase_orders, book_transfers, ill_requests — no rule at
    all, so the gate log, acquisitions, transfers and ILL screens silently
    did nothing
  - _sync/heartbeat, so the Android WorkManager sync job failed every run
  - settings writes, even by the institution's own owner
  - directorate_index reads AND writes, which is why the directorate
    dashboard could never show anything

The root cause of the settings denial was isCollegeDirector() calling `.data`
on a get() of /colleges/{id} — a registry document the desktop onboarding flow
never creates. get() returns null for a missing document and null.data raises.
Books, members, loans and reservations remained writable, because Firestore
tolerates an evaluation error in one branch of a `||` when another branch
allows; settings had no such second branch. Every get() is now guarded by
exists(), and optional map keys are read via .get(key, default) so a profile
without a collegeIds field cannot raise either.

A catch-all match now covers tenant sub-collections so a collection added on
one platform can no longer be silently unwritable on another. It explicitly
excludes settings and audit_log: Firestore rules are a union rather than
first-match, so without that exclusion the catch-all would re-grant exactly
what those stricter rules restrict.

DIRECTORATE REGISTRY

Three registries had grown up in parallel and disagreed: /colleges (web
onboarding), /directorate_index (Android and the desktop profile screen), and
profile fields on /institutions. The web director page read /colleges filtered
on a directorUid nothing ever wrote, and rendered collegeName where onboarding
had written name — hence blank rows even before the rules denied the read.

/directorate_index is now canonical: one aggregate-only document per college,
published by the clients since the Spark plan has no Cloud Functions. It holds
counts and a heartbeat, never patron or catalogue records, which is what makes
it safe to expose network-wide while tenant data stays isolated. All three
platforms publish it (registry_service.py, DirectorateRegistry.kt,
directorate.ts) and the reader normalises every historical field spelling so
colleges on older builds still appear. /colleges remains as a legacy mirror.

The desktop directorate dashboard now reads those published counts instead of
streaming every book and member document of every college on each refresh,
which was O(all documents in the network) per refresh against a free-tier read
quota.

DIRECTOR PORTAL

New read-only /director route in web-app with its own shell and access gate,
separate from /dashboard, which is scoped to one institution. Network KPIs,
sortable and searchable college registry, per-college drill-down, CSV export.
Falls back to enumerating /institutions when no snapshot has been published
yet, so a freshly upgraded deployment sees its network rather than an empty
screen. The portal's account is deliberately not a member of any college, so
patron data stays closed to it by the rules and not merely by the UI.

ANDROID

Five paths substituted the hardcoded institution "gdc11" when the real one
could not be resolved. That id belongs to a real college, so the result was not
an inert failure — the device attached to another institution's data:

  SettingsViewModel.triggerSync    assigned it unconditionally, so pressing
                                   "Sync Now" discarded the institution login
                                   had just resolved
  SettingsViewModel.resetDatabase  fell back to it before wiping every cloud
                                   document it could reach
  DashboardViewModel               captured the id before login had written it
                                   and pinned the 2-minute background sync to
                                   the fallback for the rest of the session
  FirebaseSyncWorker               same fallback in the WorkManager job
  CollegeProfileViewModel          published this college's profile into it

RealtimeSyncManager.syncStatus was declared as
    val syncStatus: StateFlow<SyncStatus> = MutableStateFlow(SyncStatus.Offline)
which discards the mutable reference at construction, so nothing could emit
into it and the badge read "Offline" permanently. It now mirrors the engine's
status.

FirebaseSyncClient recovered from every authentication failure into its offline
demo path, so a wrong password signed the user in against "gdc-peshawar" — an
apparent success followed by an app that synced nothing. That path is now
reachable only when no Firebase project is configured. registerCollege also
created every college as "gdc11" rather than the id the user chose.

WEB

  - the header's "Real-time Sync Active" dot was hardcoded green, so a session
    whose writes were being rejected still looked healthy; it now reflects the
    listener state and surfaces errors
  - removed the button that overwrote the user's institutionId with "gdc11"
  - the AI recommender read a collection named "issues" that nothing writes
  - loan counts tested a `returned` boolean no platform writes, so returned
    loans counted as both active and overdue; both now use the canonical
    status field via shared predicates in lib/schema.ts
  - listener errors are surfaced instead of leaving a silently empty table

Also fixes a pre-existing type error in the enterprise page that was breaking
`next build`, and adds indexes for the queries the sync engines actually run.

