From c87c79fb1e2a5cda15213608d97b96d818fe1314 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:05:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Sync library policy across platforms and reach the search
 screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SETTINGS DID NOT SYNC

Fine rate, loan period and borrowing limit were stored three different ways:

  Android   SharedPreferences only, fine rate defaulting to 1.0, never
            uploaded or downloaded
  Desktop   wrote only fineRatePerDay to Firestore, defaulting to 5.0
  Web       a local useState with a hardcoded 5.0, no save button wired to
            anything, so the page persisted nothing at all

The consequence is that the same overdue book produced a different fine
depending on which device processed the return, and changing the rate on one
platform had no effect on the others.

There is now one document, /institutions/{id}/settings/library_settings,
described by LibrarySettings in three parallel implementations that share the
same field names and the same fine arithmetic:

  shared/.../data/model/LibrarySettings.kt
  gdc_desktop/services/settings_service.py
  web-app/src/lib/settings.ts

It adds a grace period and a per-loan cap alongside the existing fields.
Android observes the document and mirrors it into SharedPreferences, which is
what keeps CalculateFineUseCase synchronous and correct offline. The desktop
app's save_settings no longer overwrites the document with only fineRatePerDay,
which had been discarding the loan period and borrowing limit written by the
other two platforms.

Four desktop sites computed `days * rate` inline, ignoring the grace period and
cap; they now route through the shared fine_for(). That was equivalent only
while both were unset, so this is what makes the new fields real rather than
decorative.

The web settings page is rewritten to actually work: it loads and saves the
policy, shows a worked example of the resulting fine, reports when a save is
rejected because the role cannot change policy, and lists real audit entries
in place of a hardcoded "No Recent Audit Logs Found". Its "Reset Database"
button previously ran confirm() and then did nothing whichever way the user
answered; rather than wire a one-click irreversible wipe of a college's
catalogue into a web page with no backup step, that action stays in the desktop
app, which backs up first, and the panel now says so. A data integrity check
that reconciles open loans against the catalogue replaces the placeholder.

CalculateFineUseCaseTest is updated for the new keys and defaults — it stubbed
getFloat("fine_per_day", 1.0f) and would have failed against the shared default
— and extended to cover the grace period, the cap, and unparseable dates. The
arithmetic is verified identical across the Kotlin and Python implementations.

UNREACHABLE SCREEN

GlobalSearchScreen is a complete, Hilt-wired screen with barcode lookup that
had no navigation route at all, so nothing could open it. Added the route and a
search action in the dashboard toolbar.

