From 36147eb0d4e745016b0f5311f8fa2ffa98b55c59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:29:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Bring all three apps to feature parity on shared
 collections
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The three apps were meant to be one institution sharing one dataset, but the
features outside the core catalogue and circulation loop were not shared at all.
On the desktop, the gate log, acquisitions, serials, ILL and transfers were
stored in local SQLite ONLY and never reached Firestore. On the web they were
Firestore-backed under camelCase names. Android had none of them. So the same
concepts lived in different places under different names, and none of it
replicated.

SHARED SCHEMA

data/model/Operations.kt, services/operations_service.py and lib/schema.ts now
describe the same nine collections with the same camelCase fields and the same
sync envelope (syncId as the document id, collegeId, lastUpdated, deleted,
syncStatus): visitor_log, purchase_orders, book_transfers, serials,
ill_requests, wishlist, reading_room, lost_found and library_events.

The desktop's local columns are partly snake_case (visitor_log.entry_time,
book_transfers.from_college) and are mapped on the way in and out.
OperationsService.migrate_local_tables copies existing local rows into
Firestore once, matched on a natural key so re-running it cannot duplicate —
without it, every record an installed desktop app had accumulated would have
stayed invisible to the other two apps.

ANDROID — seven new screens

Gate log, acquisitions, book transfers, serials, ILL, MARC catalogue and
enterprise, plus a shared OperationsCommon for the chrome these six list-plus-
workflow screens have in common.

They go through OperationsRepository rather than SQLDelight and the KMP sync
engine, deliberately: adding six entities to SQLDelight means schema migrations
on a deployed app, and the Firestore Android SDK already enables offline
persistence by default, so reads come from the local cache and writes are
queued and replayed on reconnect.

MARC generation is extracted from BookDetailScreen into data/marc/Marc21.kt.
It was written inline there, and the new catalogue screen needs the same
output — two copies of a cataloguing format drift apart.

DESKTOP

Sync network pairing (previously web-only, so a Windows-only librarian could
not join the college network at all) and a wishlist screen matching Android's.
Approving a wishlist title offers to raise the purchase order rather than
making the librarian retype it in Acquisitions.

The five local-only screens now read and write the shared collections, falling
back to their local tables when offline or signed out so the desk keeps working
either way.

WEB

Serials, which the sidebar linked to "#" and marked WIP, and Wishlist, which
did not exist. Both mirror the desktop and Android behaviour, including the
rule that adding an already-requested title upvotes it instead of creating a
duplicate — that count is what tells an acquisitions librarian which titles are
actually in demand, so the three implementations must agree on what counts as
the same title.

Enterprise seats, lost property and events are backed by real collections. The
desktop and web panels both held them in memory only, so an occupied seat
vanished on refresh and no other device ever saw it.

Verified: next build passes; operations_service unit-tested against a fake
Firestore for envelope shape, computed PO totals, wishlist dedupe and upvote,
soft-delete retaining the document, seat reassignment, ILL fulfilment stamping
and offline no-op behaviour. Android and PyQt6 are not compile-verified here —
no Android SDK or PyQt6 in this environment.

